"ATTest: Agent-Driven Tensor Testing for Deep Learning Library Modules | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/abs/2602.13987)\nciteturn31903search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; @misc{zhan2026attestagentdriventensortesting, title={ATTest: Agent-Driven Tensor Testing for Deep Learning Library Modules}, author={Zhengyu Zhan and Ye Shang and Jiawei Liu and Chunrong Fang and Quanjun Zhang and Zhenyu Chen}, year={2026}, eprint={2602.13987}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.SE}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.13987}, }\n## The Agentic Framework: A Seven-Stage Workflow\n\nATTEST moves away from linear pipelines by implementing an agent-driven lifecycle. The framework decomposes the testing process into seven distinct stages, which are managed by a supervisory agent that can dynamically backtrack or repeat steps based on the current system state.\n\n  1. Understand Function: The agent inspects the target API's signature, documentation, and source code to build a semantic context.\n  2. Generate Requirements: The agent derives specific constraints (e.g., \"input must be a 4D tensor\") and identifies edge cases.\n  3. Design Test Plan: Specifications are encoded into a `test_plan.json` file. This plan categorizes tests into a $\\text{SMOKE\\_SET}$SMOKE_SET for rapid validation and a $\\text{DEFERRED\\_SET}$DEFERRED_SET for more exhaustive exploration.\n  4. Generate Code: The agent synthesizes unit tests organized into isolated blocks (e.g., $\\text{CASE}_*$CASE∗​).\n  5. Execute Tests: Tests are run in isolated subprocesses using standard tools like `pytest`.\n  6. Analyze Results: The agent processes execution failures and tracebacks, producing a structured decision artifact (`analysis_plan.json`) to guide repairs.\n  7. Generate Report: A final consolidation of achieved coverage and diagnostic information is created.\n\nThis decomposition allows the system to focus on task-relevant information at each step, mitigating the risk of the LLM becoming \"lost in the middle\" of a massive context window.\n\n## Engineering Mechanisms for Stability\n\nA core contribution of ATTEST is its focus on engineering stability during the iterative repair process. The framework employs several mechanisms to prevent the common pitfalls of LLM-driven development.\n\n### Incremental Block-Level Editing\n\nTo prevent destructive rewrites, ATTEST divides test files into semantically isolated blocks. When a test failure is detected, the agent is restricted to modifying only the relevant $\\text{CASE}_*$CASE∗​ or $\\text{HEADER}$HEADER blocks. By localizing the scope of changes, the framework ensures that previously validated test logic remains intact, significantly increasing the reliability of the synthesis process.\n\n### Persistent State Management\n\nTesting complex libraries is a long-horizon task. ATTEST maintains a unified state file that serializes all intermediate artifacts, decisions, and tool outputs. This persistence ensures that the workflow can be resumed after interruptions and that the supervisory agent has access to the full history of successful and failed attempts, which is critical for avoiding repetitive errors.\n\n### Selective Log Ingestion\n\nStandard execution logs for deep learning operations can be extremely verbose, containing lengthy stack traces and system environment details. Rather than feeding the entire log into the LLM, ATTEST uses specialized search and partial-reading tools to extract only the error-relevant fragments. This reduces token consumption and provides the agent with a clearer diagnostic signal for repair.\n\n## Citation\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{zhan2026attestagentdriventensortesting, title={ATTest: Agent-Driven Tensor Testing for Deep Learning Library Modules}, author={Zhengyu Zhan and Ye Shang and Jiawei Liu and Chunrong Fang and Quanjun Zhang and Zhenyu Chen}, year={2026}, eprint={2602.13987}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={cs.SE}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.13987}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nATTest: Agent-Driven Tensor Testing for Deep Learning... (https://arxivlens.com/paperview/details/attest-agent-driven-tensor-testing-for-deep-learning-library-modules-3478-4588daea)\nciteturn31903search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: yesterday; 2602.13987 Published Feb 15, 2026 ... Source code: https://github.com/iSEngLab/ATTest.git\n\nBack ArXiv cs.SE ATTest: Agent-Driven Tensor Testing for Deep Learning Library Modules\n\nArXiv cs.SE\n\n# ATTest: Agent-Driven Tensor Testing for Deep Learning Library Modules\n\nChunrong Fang, Jiawei Liu, Quanjun Zhang, Ye Shang, Zhengyu Zhan, Zhenyu Chen\n\n2602.13987 Published Feb 15, 2026\n\nChat with paper Cite 中文 View PDF Share\n\n### Publication\n\nPublished: Feb 15, 2026\n\nCategories: cs.SE\n\n### Metrics\n\nSource: ArXiv\n\n### Paper Preview\n\n### Abstract\n\nThe unit testing of Deep Learning (DL) libraries is challenging due to complex numerical semantics and implicit tensor constraints. Traditional Search-Based Software Testing (SBST) often suffers from semantic blindness, failing to satisfy the constraints of high-dimensional tensors, whereas Large Language Models (LLMs) struggle with cross-file context and unstable code modifications. This paper proposes ATTest, an agent-driven tensor testing framework for module-level unit test generation. ATTest orchestrates a seven-stage pipeline, which encompasses constraint extraction and an iterative \"generation-validation-repair\" loop, to maintain testing stability and mitigate context-window saturation. An evaluation on PyTorch and TensorFlow demonstrates that ATTest significantly outperforms state-of-the-art baselines such as PynguinML, achieving an average branch coverage of 55.60% and 54.77%, respectively. The results illustrate how agent-driven workflows bridge the semantic gap in numerical libraries while ensuring auditable test synthesis. Source code: https://github.com/iSEngLab/ATTest.git\n\n### Paper Details\n\nPaper ID: 2602.13987\n\nComments: 5 pages, 3 figures\n\nCategories:\n\ncs.SE\n\nView on arXiv Download PDF\n\n### Authors\n\nCF Chunrong Fang 108 papers JL Jiawei Liu 6 papers QZ Quanjun Zhang 3 papers YS Ye Shang 2 papers ZZ Zhengyu Zhan ZC Zhenyu Chen 70 papers--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSearch | arXiv e-print repository (https://quickbooks-ai.org/?_=%2Fsearch%2Fcs%23zUXcvxMRZtzs00ASQPF%2B0sI%3D&query=Wu%2C+N&searchtype=author)\nciteturn31903search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; While prior methods accelerate sampling by caching intermediate features, they rely on static reuse schedules or coarse-grained heuristics, which often lead to temporal drift and cache misalignment that signif… ▽ More Diffusion Transformers (DiTs) achieve state-of-the-art performance in high-fidelity image and video generation but suffer from expensive inference due to their iterative denoising structure. ... At each timestep, AdaCorrection estimates cache validity with lightweight spatio-temporal signals and adaptively blends cached and fresh activations. ... Submitted 7 May, 2026; v1 submitted 13 February, 2026; originally announced February 2026.\nWhile prior methods accelerate sampling by caching intermediate features, they rely on static reuse schedules or coarse-grained heuristics, which often lead to temporal drift and cache misalignment that significantly degrade generation quality. We introduce \\textbf{AdaCorrection}, an adaptive offset cache correction framework that maintains high generation fidelity while enabling efficient cache reuse across Transformer layers during diffusion inference. At each timestep, AdaCorrection estimates cache validity with lightweight spatio-temporal signals and adaptively blends cached and fresh activations. This correction is computed on-the-fly without additional supervision or retraining. Our approach achieves strong generation quality with minimal computational overhead, maintaining near-original FID while providing moderate acceleration. Experiments on image and video diffusion benchmarks show that AdaCorrection consistently improves generation performance. △ Less\n\nSubmitted 7 May, 2026; v1 submitted 13 February, 2026; originally announced February 2026.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Quanjun Zhang (https://dblp.org/pid/231/4867.html)\nciteturn31903search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month;       * journals/corr/abs-2602-13987 ... ATTest: Agent-Driven Tensor Testing for Deep Learning Library Modules.\n\nZhengyu Zhan, Ye Shang, Jiawei Liu, Chunrong Fang, Quanjun Zhang, Zhenyu Chen:\nATTest: Agent-Driven Tensor Testing for Deep Learning Library Modules. CoRR abs/2602.13987 (2026)\n  * Image: Informal and Other Publications\n\n[i46]\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI (open access)\n      * details & citations\n\nauthority control:\n\n      *  \n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2602-23047\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ndblp: Ye Shang (https://dblp.org/pid/387/2432)\nciteturn31903search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 4 months ago;       * journals/corr/abs-2602-13987 ... ATTest: Agent-Driven Tensor Testing for Deep Learning Library Modules.\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2602-13987\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n      * Bluesky\n      * Reddit\n      * BibSonomy\n      * LinkedIn\n\npersistent URL:\n\n      * https://dblp.org/rec/journals/corr/abs-2602-13987\n\nZhengyu Zhan, Ye Shang, Jiawei Liu, Chunrong Fang, Quanjun Zhang, Zhenyu Chen:\nATTest: Agent-Driven Tensor Testing for Deep Learning Library Modules. CoRR abs/2602.13987 (2026)\n  * Image: Informal and Other Publications\n\n[i9]\n\n    * view\n\n      * electronic edition via DOI (open access)\n      * details & citations\n\nauthority control:\n\n      *  \n\n    * export record\n\n      * BibTeX\n      * RIS\n      * RDF N-Triples\n      * RDF Turtle\n      * RDF/XML\n      * XML\n      * plain text\n\ndblp key:\n\n      * journals/corr/abs-2602-23047\n\n    * ask others\n\n      * Google\n      * Google Scholar\n      * Semantic Scholar\n      * Internet Archive Scholar\n      * CiteSeerX\n      * PubPeer\n\n    * share record\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDual-Signal Adaptive KV-Cache Optimization for Long-Form Video Understanding in Vision-Language Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14236)\nciteturn31903academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Date: Sun Feb 15 17:06:02 2026 ... Furthermore, under identical memory budget constraints, Sali-Cache preserves context-rich features over extended temporal durations without degrading model performance, enabling efficient processing of long-form video content on consumer-grade hardware.\nTitle: Dual-Signal Adaptive KV-Cache Optimization for Long-Form Video Understanding in Vision-Language Models\nAuthors: Vishnu Sai, Dheeraj Sai, Srinath B, Girish Varma, Priyesh Shukla\nDate: Sun Feb 15 17:06:02 2026\n\nVision-Language Models (VLMs) face a critical memory bottleneck when processing long-form video content due to the linear growth of the Key-Value (KV) cache with sequence length. Existing solutions predominantly employ reactive eviction strategies that compute full attention matrices before discarding tokens, resulting in substantial computational waste. We propose Sali-Cache, a novel a priori optimization framework that implements dual-signal adaptive caching through proactive memory management. By integrating a temporal filter based on optical flow analysis for detecting inter-frame redundancy and a spatial filter leveraging saliency detection for identifying visually significant regions, Sali-Cache intelligently manages memory allocation before entering computationally expensive attention operations. Experimental evaluation on the LLaVA 1.6 architecture demonstrates that our method achieves a 2.20x compression ratio in effective memory usage while maintaining 100% accuracy across BLEU, ROUGE-L, and Exact Match metrics. Furthermore, under identical memory budget constraints, Sali-Cache preserves context-rich features over extended temporal durations without degrading model performance, enabling efficient processing of long-form video content on consumer-grade hardware.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDaily Papers - Hugging Face (https://huggingface.co/papers?q=theoretical+guidance)\nciteturn31903search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Feb 25, 2021 ... We then develop theory describing edit dynamics under (i) noise-injection and denoising transport, (ii) inversion-and-edit pipelines and the propagation of inversion errors, and (iii) locality constraints implemented via masked guidance or hard constraints. ... Generally speaking, two families of techniques have emerged for solving this problem for gradient-based guidance: namely, posterior guidance (i.e., guidance via projecting the current sample to the target distribution via the target prediction model) and end-to-end guidance (i.e., guidance by performing backpropagation throughout the entire ODE solve). ... ### Spectral Collapse in Diffusion Inversion ... In this white paper for the Snowmass community planning process, we discuss the unique requirements of machine learning for lattice quantum field theory research and outline what is needed to enable exploration and deployment of this approach in the future.\n### Spectral Collapse in Diffusion Inversion\n\n【91†Conditional diffusion inversion provides a powerful framework for unpaired image-to-image translation. However, we demonstrate through an extensive analysis that standard deterministic inversion (e.g. DDIM) fails when the source domain is spectrally sparse compared to the target domain (e.g., super-resolution, sketch-to-image). In these contexts, the recovered latent from the input does not follow the expected isotropic Gaussian distribution. Instead it exhibits a signal with lower frequencies, locking target sampling to oversmoothed and texture-poor generations. We term this phenomenon spectral collapse. We observe that stochastic alternatives attempting to restore the noise variance tend to break the semantic link to the input, leading to structural drift. To resolve this structure-texture trade-off, we propose Orthogonal Variance Guidance (OVG), an inference-time method that corrects the ODE dynamics to enforce the theoretical Gaussian noise magnitude within the null-space of the structural gradient. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nATTest: Pruebas de tensores impulsadas por agentes para módulos de bibliotecas de aprendizaje profundo | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/es/abs/2602.13987)\nciteturn31903search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last month; ATTest orquesta una cadena de siete etapas que abarca la extracción de restricciones y un bucle iterativo de \"generación-validación-reparación\" para mantener la estabilidad de las pruebas y mitigar la saturación de la ventana de contexto. ...   2.\nSubmitted 15 Feb 2026\n\nes\n\n# ATTest: Pruebas de tensores impulsadas por agentes para módulos de bibliotecas de aprendizaje profundo\n\nZZ\n\nZhengyu Zhan\n\nYS\n\nYe Shang\n\nJL\n\nJiawei Liu\n\nCF\n\nChunrong Fang\n\nQZ\n\nQuanjun Zhang\n\nZC\n\nZhenyu Chen\n\n## Abstract\n\nLas pruebas unitarias de las bibliotecas de Deep Learning (DL) son desafiantes debido a la compleja semántica numérica y las restricciones implícitas de los tensores. Las pruebas de software basadas en búsqueda (SBST) tradicionales a menudo sufren de ceguera semántica, lo que les impide satisfacer las restricciones de los tensores de alta dimensión, mientras que los Grandes Modelos de Lenguaje (LLM) tienen dificultades con el contexto entre archivos y las modificaciones de código inestables. Este artículo propone ATTest, un marco de pruebas de tensores impulsado por agentes para la generación de pruebas unitarias a nivel de módulo. ATTest orquesta una cadena de siete etapas que abarca la extracción de restricciones y un bucle iterativo de \"generación-validación-reparación\" para mantener la estabilidad de las pruebas y mitigar la saturación de la ventana de contexto. Una evaluación en PyTorch y TensorFlow demuestra que ATTest supera significativamente a las líneas base de vanguardia, como PynguinML, logrando una cobertura de ramas promedio del 55.60% y 54.77%, respectivamente. Los resultados ilustran cómo los flujos de trabajo impulsados por agentes cierran la brecha semántica en las bibliotecas numéricas, al tiempo que garantizan una síntesis de pruebas auditable. Código fuente: este https URL\n\n## AI Overview\n\nEl desarrollo de bibliotecas modernas de Aprendizaje Profundo (DL) como PyTorch y TensorFlow ha creado un desafío fundamental para la garantía de calidad del software. Estas bibliotecas se basan en operaciones numéricas de alta dimensión donde la validez de una entrada se rige por complejas \"restricciones de tensor\", como rangos, formas y tipos de datos (dtypes) específicos. Los métodos tradicionales de pruebas automatizadas a menudo tienen dificultades para navegar por estas restricciones, lo que lleva a suites de prueba ineficaces que no logran alcanzar la lógica computacional central del software.\n\nATTEST (Agent-Driven Tensor Testing) es un marco autónomo diseñado para abordar estas limitaciones al tratar el proceso de generación de pruebas como una tarea de ingeniería agencial. En lugar de depender de un proceso de generación rígido y único, ATTEST utiliza un Modelo de Lenguaje Grande (LLM) como un agente activo que orquesta un flujo de trabajo estructurado de siete etapas. Este enfoque permite que el sistema razone sobre restricciones numéricas implícitas y refine iterativamente las pruebas unitarias basándose en la retroalimentación de la ejecución, asegurando que el código generado sea tanto sintácticamente correcto como semánticamente compatible con los requisitos de los operadores de aprendizaje profundo.\n\nImage: Figura 1: La arquitectura del marco ATTEST, que ilustra el flujo de trabajo de siete etapas, la gestión de persistencia y el registro de herramientas integrado para la generación de pruebas unitarias impulsadas por agentes.\n\n\n### Inestabilidad en la generación de LLM\n\nSi bien los LLM poseen una mejor comprensión semántica del código y la documentación, se enfrentan a su propio conjunto de desafíos. Los ciclos fijos de \"generación-validación-reparación\" a menudo conducen a una \"reescritura destructiva\", donde una corrección para una parte de un caso de prueba rompe inadvertidamente otra. Además, los LLM pueden \"alucinar\" código cuando se trata de dependencias complejas entre archivos o estados de hardware especializados (como CUDA). Sin una forma estructurada de gestionar el estado del proceso de prueba, estos modelos tienen dificultades para mantener la coherencia arquitectónica durante largas secuencias de modificaciones.\n\n## El marco agencial: un flujo de trabajo de siete etapas\n\nATTEST se aleja de los procesos lineales al implementar un ciclo de vida impulsado por agentes. El marco descompone el proceso de prueba en siete etapas distintas, que son gestionadas por un agente supervisor que puede retroceder o repetir pasos dinámicamente según el estado actual del sistema.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nATTest: エージェント駆動テンソルテスト ディープラーニングライブラリのモジュール向け | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/ja/abs/2602.13987)\nciteturn31903search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last month; # ATTest: エージェント駆動テンソルテスト ディープラーニングライブラリのモジュール向け\nSubmitted 15 Feb 2026\n\nja\n\n# ATTest: エージェント駆動テンソルテスト ディープラーニングライブラリのモジュール向け\n\nZZ\n\nZhengyu Zhan\n\nYS\n\nYe Shang\n\nJL\n\nJiawei Liu\n\nCF\n\nChunrong Fang\n\nQZ\n\nQuanjun Zhang\n\nZC\n\nZhenyu Chen\n\n## Abstract\n\n深層学習（DL）ライブラリの単体テストは、その複雑な数値意味論と暗黙的なテンソル制約のために困難である。従来の探索型ソフトウェアテスト（SBST）は、セマンティックな盲目性に陥りがちで、高次元テンソルの制約を満たすことができない。一方、大規模言語モデル（LLM）は、ファイル間のコンテキスト処理や不安定なコード修正に課題を抱えている。本稿では、モジュールレベルの単体テスト生成のためのエージェント駆動型テンソルテストフレームワーク、ATTestを提案する。ATTestは、制約抽出と反復的な「生成-検証-修正」ループを含む7段階のパイプラインを編成し、テストの安定性を維持し、コンテキストウィンドウの飽和を軽減する。\nATTEST (Agent-Driven Tensor Testing) は、テスト生成プロセスをエージェント工学のタスクとして捉えることで、これらの制限に対処するために設計された自律型フレームワークです。ATTESTは、厳格なワンショット生成パイプラインに頼るのではなく、大規模言語モデル（LLM）を能動的なエージェントとして利用し、構造化された7段階のワークフローをオーケストレーションします。このアプローチにより、システムは暗黙の数値制約について推論し、実行フィードバックに基づいて単体テストを反復的に改良することができ、生成されたコードが構文的に正しく、かつディープラーニング演算子の要件に意味的に準拠していることを保証します。\n\nImage: 図1：ATTESTフレームワークのアーキテクチャ。7段階のワークフロー、永続性管理、エージェント駆動の単体テスト生成のための統合ツールレジストリを示す。\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - hasadna/talpiot_hackathon2026_sali · GitHub (https://github.com/hasadna/talpiot_hackathon2026_sali)\nciteturn31903search8 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; hasadna / talpiot_hackathon2026_sali Public ... For the same reason the catalogue, its pool, its cache, and its breaker are process-wide (`default_catalog()`) rather than rebuilt per request.\n\nhasadna / talpiot_hackathon2026_sali Public\n\nforked from MajoRoth/sali\n\n  * Notifications You must be signed in to change notification settings\n  * Fork 0\n  * Star\n\n  * Code\n  * Pull requests 0\n  * Actions\n  * Projects\n  * Security and quality 0\n  * Insights\n\nAdditional navigation options\n\nmain\n\nBranchesTags\n\nGo to file\n\nCode\n\nOpen more actions menu\n\n \n\n \n\n## Folders and files\n\nName  | Name  | Last commit message  | Last commit date\n--- | --- | --- | ---\n\n## Latest commit\n\n \n\n## History\n\n55 Commits\n.claude  | .claude  |    |  \ndocs  | docs  |    |  \nopenapi  | openapi  |    |  \nsrc  | src  |    |  \nstatic  | static  |    |  \nsupermarket_api_backend  | supermarket_api_backend  |    |  \ntests  | tests  |    |  \nui  | ui  |    |  \n.env.example  | .env.example  |    |  \n.gitignore  | .gitignore  |    |  \nCONTEXT.md  | CONTEXT.md  |    |  \nREADME.md  | README.md  |    |  \npyproject.toml  | pyproject.toml  |    |  \nuv.lock  | uv.lock  |    |  \n[Button: View all files]\n\n# sali\n\nAnalyze and compare supermarket prices. Compare shopping carts across stores and get recommendations for cheaper alternatives.\n\nFor the same reason the catalogue, its pool, its cache, and its breaker are process-wide (`default_catalog()`) rather than rebuilt per request.\n\nAn incomplete pricing index is cached for minutes rather than hours: one built while the pricing instance was half-down would otherwise keep the branches it missed unpriceable all day, and those are exactly the branches that carry prices. The directory is also warmed on startup so the first shopper does not pay for assembling it.\n\nWhen nothing can be priced the response says so explicitly:\n    \n    `\"no store prices: the price database has no current listings for any matched product\"\n    `\n\n#### Simulated prices (development only)\n\nBecause the price database is often unable to price anything at all, the comparison screens can be run against simulated figures by setting `SALI_FALLBACK_PRICES=1`. See `src/sali/nearby/fallback.py`.\n\nThese are not real prices. They are derived from the shopper's own receipt, varied per store by a deterministic hash, purely so the screens have plausibly-shaped data to lay out. This is off unless explicitly enabled; every response it touches carries a `demo prices:` warning and every store it builds is flagged `simulated: true`, and the UI renders a non-dismissible banner and suppresses the savings celebration whenever it sees them.\n\nRanking against real data is exercised by `tests/cart_comparison/test_ranking.py` and starts returning stores as soon as listings land — no code change needed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ntalpiot_hackathon2026_sali/README.md at main · hasadna/talpiot_hackathon2026_sali · GitHub (https://github.com/hasadna/talpiot_hackathon2026_sali/blob/main/README.md)\nciteturn31903search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; `SALI_LOG_FAILURE_DETAIL`  | unset  | Set to `1` to log model- and page-derived failure text while debugging\n## Configuration\n\nVariable  | Default  | Purpose\n--- | --- | ---\n`OPENAI_API_KEY`  | —  | Required for every extraction endpoint; read from the process or `.env`\n`SALI_PRICES_API_URL`  | `https://data.openisraelisupermarkets.co.il`  | The Open Supermarkets instance prices are read from\n`SALI_GEOCODING_API_URL`  | `http://34.165.235.189:8000`  | The instance store coordinates are read from\n`SUPERMARKET_API_KEY`  | bundled public token  | Bearer token for the pricing instance\n`SALI_FALLBACK_PRICES`  | unset  | Set to `1` to simulate prices when the database is down — development only, not real prices\n`SALI_STORE_DIRECTORY_TTL_SECONDS`  | `21600`  | How long the cross-instance pricing index is cached\n`SALI_CORS_ORIGINS`  | `http://localhost:5173`  | Comma-separated browser origins allowed to call the API\n`SALI_LOG_FAILURE_DETAIL`  | unset  | Set to `1` to log model- and page-derived failure text while debugging\n\n## UI\n\nMobile-first React app in `ui/` (upload a receipt → compare the cart across nearby stores). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nATTest: डीप लर्निंग लाइब्रेरी मॉड्यूल्स के लिए एजेंट-संचालित टेंसर परीक्षण | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/hi/abs/2602.13987)\nciteturn31903search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; ATTEST के पीछे की प्रेरणा को समझने के लिए, यह जांचना आवश्यक है कि पिछले तरीके — अर्थात् सर्च-बेस्ड सॉफ्टवेयर टेस्टिंग (SBST) और मानक LLM-आधारित पीढ़ी — डीप लर्निंग मॉड्यूल पर लागू होने पर अक्सर क्यों विफल रहते हैं। ...   2.\n## मौजूदा परीक्षण दृष्टिकोणों की सीमाएँ\n\nATTEST के पीछे की प्रेरणा को समझने के लिए, यह जांचना आवश्यक है कि पिछले तरीके — अर्थात् सर्च-बेस्ड सॉफ्टवेयर टेस्टिंग (SBST) और मानक LLM-आधारित पीढ़ी — डीप लर्निंग मॉड्यूल पर लागू होने पर अक्सर क्यों विफल रहते हैं।\n\n### SBST में शब्दार्थ अंधापन\n\nपारंपरिक SBST उपकरण, जैसे Pynguin, कोड कवरेज को अधिकतम करने के लिए मेटाहेरिस्टिक खोज का उपयोग करते हैं। हालांकि, ये उपकरण टेंसर लाइब्रेरीज़ के उच्च-आयामी इनपुट स्थानों के संबंध में अक्सर \"शब्दार्थ अंधापन\" से पीड़ित होते हैं। \nATTEST एक एजेंट-संचालित जीवनचक्र को लागू करके रैखिक पाइपलाइनों से दूर चला जाता है। फ्रेमवर्क परीक्षण प्रक्रिया को सात अलग-अलग चरणों में विभाजित करता है, जिन्हें एक पर्यवेक्षी एजेंट द्वारा प्रबंधित किया जाता है जो वर्तमान सिस्टम स्थिति के आधार पर गतिशील रूप से पीछे हट सकता है या चरणों को दोहरा सकता है।\n\n  1. फ़ंक्शन को समझना: एजेंट एक अर्थपूर्ण संदर्भ बनाने के लिए लक्ष्य एपीआई के हस्ताक्षर, दस्तावेज़ और स्रोत कोड का निरीक्षण करता है।\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nATTest : Tests de tenseurs pilotés par des agents pour les modules de bibliothèques d'apprentissage profond | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/fr/abs/2602.13987)\nciteturn31903search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: last month; Plutôt que de transmettre l'intégralité du journal au LLM, ATTEST utilise des outils de recherche et de lecture partielle spécialisés pour extraire uniquement les fragments pertinents pour l'erreur.\nLe cadre a été comparé à PynguinML, une version spécialisée de l'outil de test basé sur la recherche à la pointe de la technologie.\n\n### Résultats de la Couverture Structurelle\n\nLa métrique principale de l'évaluation était la couverture des branches, qui mesure le nombre de chemins de flux de contrôle dans le code source qui sont exercés par les tests générés.\n\n  * PyTorch: ATTEST a atteint une couverture moyenne des branches de 55,60 %, contre 43,13 % pour la référence.\n  * TensorFlow: ATTEST a atteint 54,77 %, tandis que la référence a enregistré 39,72 %.\n  * Globalement: Sur les deux bibliothèques, ATTEST a atteint 55,19 % de couverture, surpassant significativement les 41,43 % atteints par la référence.\n\n### Analyse de la Couverture Relative\n\nPour normaliser les résultats entre des modules de complexité variable, les chercheurs ont utilisé une métrique de couverture relative $\\text{cov}_r(s, e)$cov r​(s,e) :\n\n$$\\text{cov}_r(s, e) = \\frac{\\text{cov}(s, e) - \\min(\\text{cov}(s))}{\\max(\\text{cov}(s)) - \\min(\\text{cov}(s))}$$cov r​(s,e)=max(cov(s))−min(cov(s))cov(s,e)−min(cov(s))​\n\nCette métrique indique à quel point la performance d'un outil est proche de la meilleure couverture observée pour un sujet donné. ATTEST a atteint $\\text{cov}_r = 1.0000$cov r​=1.0000 sur une grande majorité des modules évalués. Cette performance démontre qu'ATTEST est constamment capable d'atteindre la couverture maximale possible pour la plupart des opérateurs, y compris les cas complexes comme les couches convolutives et les routines de quantification où les formes et les types de tenseurs doivent être parfaitement alignés.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDual-Signal Adaptive KV-Cache Optimization for (https://thrlvemurket.com/?_=%2Fpdf%2F2602.14236%232LHwtQTrZ4XkLaecG94sHNc%3D)\nciteturn31903search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; We introduce Sali-Cache, a framework that fundamentally ... arXiv:2602.14236v1  [cs.CV]  15 Feb 2026\nmechanism. Our approach leverages two complementary sig-\nnals:\nAbstract—Vision-Language Models (VLMs) face a critical\nmemory bottleneck when processing long-form video content due\nto the linear growth of the Key-Value (KV) cache with sequence\nlength. Existing solutions predominantly employ reactive eviction\nstrategies that compute full attention matrices before discarding\ntokens, resulting in substantial computational waste. We propose\nSali-Cache, a novel a priori optimization framework that imple-\nments dual-signal adaptive caching through proactive memory\nmanagement. By integrating a temporal filter based on optical\nflow analysis for detecting inter-frame redundancy and a spatial\nfilter leveraging saliency detection for identifying visually signifi-\ncant regions, Sali-Cache intelligently manages memory allocation\nbefore entering computationally expensive attention operations.\nExperimental evaluation on the LLaVA 1.6 architecture demon-\nstrates that our method achieves a 2.20× compression ratio in\neffective memory usage while maintaining 100% accuracy across\nBLEU, ROUGE-L, and Exact Match metrics. Furthermore,\nunder identical memory budget constraints, Sali-Cache preserves\ncontext-rich features over extended temporal durations without\ndegrading model performance, enabling efficient processing of\nlong-form video content on consumer-grade hardware.\nIndex Terms—Vision-Language Models, KV-Cache Optimiza-\ntion, Video Understanding, Memory Efficiency, Attention Mech-\nanisms\nI. INTRODUCTION\n1) Temporal Filtering: Optical flow-based detection of\ninter-frame redundancy, enabling cache reuse for tem-\nporally similar frames.\n2) Spatial Filtering: Saliency-guided quantization that pre-\nserves high-precision representations for visually signifi-\ncant regions while aggressively compressing background\nelements.\nOur contributions are summarized as follows:\n• We propose Sali-Cache, a dual-signal adaptive caching\nframework that optimizes KV-cache memory usage\nthrough proactive, pre-attention filtering.\n• We demonstrate a 2.20× memory compression ratio\nwhile maintaining 100% accuracy on standard evaluation\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLINE: A Learned Index with Group-Enhanced Leaves and (https://www.shimin-chen.com/papers/LINE-pacmmod26.pdf)\nciteturn31903search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; with two Intel Xeon Gold 5218 CPUs (2.30GHz, 16 cores/32 threads, 22MB L3 cache) and 348GB ... synchronization [19]; 4) ALEX+, a multi-threaded implementation of ALEX [43]; and 5) SALI, a ... Publication date: June 2026.\n"
